<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<PackageManifest Version="2.0.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema/2011"
                xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema-design/2011">
  <Metadata>
    <Identity Id="DeepSeekHarness.VisualStudio" Version="0.2.2" Language="en-US" Publisher="VSworder" />
    <DisplayName>DeepSeek Harness for Visual Studio</DisplayName>
    <!-- The schema caps this element at 1000 characters after entity decoding, and the cap
         is on the parsed value, not on the XML. Keep it tight. -->
    <Description xml:space="preserve">Community-built and unofficial: not affiliated with DeepSeek. Review DeepSeek Harness file edits in Visual Studio's native diff window before they touch disk.

DeepSeek Harness (dsh) is a terminal agent. This extension gives it the one thing a terminal cannot: a real diff. When the agent wants to change a file, the diff opens beside your code &#8212; left is what is on disk, right is what the model proposes &#8212; with Accept and Reject. Nothing is written until you accept, and a rejection carries your reason back to the model.

Setup is just the VSIX. Nothing is added to your DeepSeek Harness profile, and nothing is copied into your repository.

Uninstalling removes every capability, because without Visual Studio there is no bridge and no diff window. What remains is a folder under %LOCALAPPDATA%. Tools &gt; DeepSeek Harness &gt; Clean up installed files removes it, and is optional.</Description>
    <MoreInfo>https://github.com/VSworder/deepseek-harness-visual-studio</MoreInfo>
    <License>LICENSE.txt</License>
    <Tags>deepseek, dsh, agent, ai, diff, llm, coding assistant</Tags>
    <!-- The Marketplace shows a preview badge from this element. The checkbox on the
         publisher portal only applies to extensions listed by link rather than uploaded as
         a VSIX, so for an uploaded one this is the only place it can be set. -->
    <Preview>true</Preview>
  </Metadata>
  <Installation>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Community" Version="[17.14,19.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
  </Installation>
  <Dependencies>
    <Dependency Id="Microsoft.Framework.NDP" DisplayName="Microsoft .NET Framework" d:Source="Manual" Version="[4.7.2,)" />
  </Dependencies>
  <Prerequisites>
    <Prerequisite Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.CoreEditor" Version="[17.14,19.0)" DisplayName="Visual Studio core editor" />
  </Prerequisites>
  <Assets>
    <!-- PkgdefProjectOutputGroup is what the VSSDK generates from the package's
         [Guid] and registration attributes; the template uses the same asset. -->
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.VsPackage" d:Source="File" Path="DeepSeekHarness.pkgdef" />
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.MefComponent" d:Source="File" Path="DeepSeekHarness.VS.dll" />
  </Assets>
</PackageManifest>
